# Design-Space Exploration of Out-of-Order Core Microarchitecture

Microarchitectural performance analysis using **gem5** — exploring how Reorder
Buffer (ROB) size and issue width affect IPC across compute-, memory-, and
branch-heavy workloads, with bottleneck analysis derived from gem5's internal
statistics.

## Summary

- Built a custom gem5 SE-mode configuration exposing ROB size and issue width
  as sweep parameters (not available in gem5's default example scripts)
- Wrote 3 workloads targeting distinct performance-limiting behaviors:
  compute-bound (matrix multiply), memory-bound (pointer-chasing linked list),
  and branch-bound (recursive quicksort)
- Ran a 36-configuration design-space sweep (4 ROB sizes x 3 issue widths x 3
  workloads) on gem5's `X86O3CPU` out-of-order model
- Diagnosed *why* IPC plateaus or behaves non-monotonically using gem5's
  ROB-full events, branch-squash counts, and memory-order-violation stats —
  not just IPC curves alone

## Key findings

| Workload | Result |
|---|---|
| Compute-heavy | Issue width dominates; ROB size stops mattering once "large enough" (~1.5% IPC change from ROB 32->192, vs. 71% from issue width 1->4) |
| Memory-heavy | IPC nearly flat (~0.091-0.092) across the entire grid — the pointer-chasing dependency chain has no independent memory-level parallelism (MLP) for OoO hardware to exploit |
| Branch-heavy | Non-monotonic: IPC peaks at ROB=64 then *drops* at ROB=128/192, because a larger ROB lets the core speculate further past unresolved branches, increasing the cost of each misprediction squash |

![IPC vs ROB size across workloads](report_assets/ipc_vs_rob.png)

## Repository structure

```
.
|-- rob_iw_config.py        # Custom gem5 SE-mode config (ROB / issue-width sweep parameters)
|-- run_sweep.sh             # Automates the 12-config sweep per workload
|-- analyze_sweep.py         # Parses stats.txt -> IPC plots + bottleneck summary CSV
|-- workloads/
|   |-- compute_heavy.c
|   |-- memory_heavy.c
|   `-- branch_heavy.c
|-- report_assets/
|   |-- ipc_vs_rob.png
|   `-- bottleneck_summary.csv
`-- OoO_DSE_Report.docx      # Full write-up
```

## Methodology

- **Simulator:** gem5 v25.1.0.1, X86 ISA, Syscall Emulation (SE) mode
- **CPU models:** `X86MinorCPU` (in-order baseline) and `X86O3CPU` (out-of-order, swept)
- **Swept parameters:** ROB size (32/64/128/192), issue width (1/2/4), matched
  across fetch/decode/rename/dispatch/commit stages
- **Metrics extracted per run:** IPC, `ROBFullEvents`, branch squash count,
  memory-order violations, fetch stall cycles

## Running it yourself

```bash
# Build workloads
gcc -static -O0 workloads/compute_heavy.c -o compute_heavy.bin
gcc -static -O0 workloads/memory_heavy.c -o memory_heavy.bin
gcc -static -O0 workloads/branch_heavy.c -o branch_heavy.bin

# Run the full sweep for each workload
./run_sweep.sh compute_heavy.bin compute
./run_sweep.sh memory_heavy.bin memory
./run_sweep.sh branch_heavy.bin branch

# Generate plots + bottleneck summary
python3 analyze_sweep.py
```

## Limitations / future work

- Power and area were not modeled with McPAT; ROB utilization is used as a
  structural proxy rather than a measured power/area figure
- One benchmark per workload category; a broader benchmark suite (e.g. a
  SPEC CPU2017 subset) would strengthen generality
- Future extension: integrate McPAT, sweep cache configuration, test
  additional branch predictors against the ROB/misprediction-penalty tradeoff
  found here
